# {{PROJECT_NAME}}

A real-time Swift backend scaffold: a shared gRPC/Protobuf contract, an
adaptive poller, an actor-based TTL cache, and an `AsyncStream` broadcaster.
See `Sources/App/Services/` for the four pieces and the comments in each
file for why they're shaped the way they are.

## Run it

```bash
swift build
swift run Run
```

`GET /health` should respond `ok`. The gRPC server task logs a "not wired
up yet" message until you complete the codegen step below — everything else
(cache, broadcaster, poller) is real, working Swift you can already read,
test, and modify.

## Next steps

1. **Fill in `Sources/App/Protos/{{ENTITY_LOWER}}.proto`** with your actual
   domain fields (it currently has a minimal `id`/`status`/`updated_at`
   shape as a starting point).
2. **Generate Swift types from it.** Install `protoc` and the grpc-swift
   plugin (`brew install swift-protobuf grpc-swift`, or see
   https://github.com/grpc/grpc-swift for the current recommended setup —
   grpc-swift 2.x favors a build-time SwiftPM plugin over manual codegen,
   check their docs for the latest approach), then generate the message and
   service types.
3. **Implement the generated service protocol**, delegating to the
   `{{ENTITY}}Broadcaster` already in this scaffold for the streaming RPC —
   `broadcaster.subscribe(channelID:)` gives you an `AsyncStream` to `yield`
   straight into the gRPC response stream.
4. **Replace `Example{{ENTITY}}Source`** in `configure.swift` with a real
   implementation of `{{ENTITY}}Source` that talks to your actual upstream
   (API, database, device gateway...). The three methods
   (`isActive`/`nextKnownEvent`/`fetchUpdates`) are the only surface the
   poller depends on.
5. **Wire the real `GRPCServer`** into `Sources/Run/main.swift` where the
   `TODO` comment is, passing your generated service provider.
6. **Tune `AdaptivePoller.Intervals`** in `configure.swift` to your domain
   — how fast is "active", how far out does "scheduled" start mattering.

## Why these four pieces

See `references/patterns.md` in the skill this scaffold came from
(`swift-realtime-backend`) for the reasoning behind each pattern — actor
isolation for shared mutable state, `AsyncStream` per subscriber instead of
a message broker, the adaptive-interval formula, and running HTTP + gRPC as
sibling structured tasks.
